{"schema":"windmark.kiteshape/1","kind":"surface","name":"AC33 benchmark asymmetric spinnaker, flying shape measured in a twisted-flow wind tunnel at AWA 55 deg","source":{"citation":"Arredondo-Galeana A., Viola I. M. (2020). PIV dataset for near wake of spinnaker: Vortex flows of downwind sails [dataset]. University of Edinburgh, School of Engineering. https://doi.org/10.7488/ds/2857, file Geometries/IGS_format/Spinnaker_high_twist_16deg.igs. Geometry: Viola I. M., Flay R. G. J. (2011), Sail aerodynamics: understanding pressure distributions on upwind sails / downwind sails, Ocean Engineering 38; Arredondo-Galeana A. (2019), PhD thesis, University of Edinburgh.","url":"https://datashare.ed.ac.uk/handle/10283/3676","licence":"CC-BY-4.0","method":"the flying shape of an AC33-class asymmetric spinnaker measured by photogrammetry in the Auckland twisted-flow wind tunnel at its optimum AWA of 55 deg, built as a rigid 1:106 model (16 deg twist foot to head); the outer face of that model's IGES geometry, resampled by boatgen.kite_surface (rows: stripes at equal shares of the luff and the leech from the foot; columns: equal shares of each row's girth from the luff)","sail":"AC33 A-sail, designed area 510 m2 full size; model span 300 mm"},"definitions":{"points":"rows from the foot (h = row / (rows - 1): the row's stripe, a share of the luff and of the leech) of columns from the luff (uc = column / (cols - 1): a share of the row's girth); [a, l, n] in model millimetres from the tack: l toward the head, a square to it toward the clew in the tack-head-clew plane, n square to both on the belly's (leeward) side","shapes":"the run is the measured reach shape turned whole by the trim and a little deeper (depth); the reach has half its foot sag below the tack-clew line lifted out (foot_lift)"},"surfaces":{"ac33":{"form":"grid","points":[[[0.0,0.0,-0.0],[3.592,-0.455,3.21],[7.297,-0.74,6.308],[11.114,-0.846,9.281],[15.038,-0.762,12.11],[19.065,-0.477,14.777],[23.186,0.019,17.262],[